  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday) Issued at 1110 AM CDT Wed Jul 1 \n2026\n\nAs we head into the 4th of July weekend, the upper-level ridge\nwill continue to weaken and move towards the east. This will lower\nthe temperatures slightly throughout the forecast period, though\nthe heat will still be present with afternoon heat indexes\nremaining near or slightly above 100 through at least Monday. As\nwe head into Monday, an upper-level trough will begin to swing\nthrough the Mississippi River Valley, providing the first\nmeaningful relief from this extended period of intense heat as\ntemperatures will begin to fall near climatological norms. \n\nFor the 4th of July, afternoon and evening showers and\nthunderstorms will be possible, potentially impacting any outdoor\nactivities. The coverage of these pop-up storms should decrease\nthe further we get from sunset on Saturday. As the upper-level\nridge loses its influence over the area, shower and\nthunderstorm chances will begin to increase heading into Sunday.\nAs the aforementioned upper-level trough begins to swing near our\nregion on Monday, widespread rain and thunderstorm chances will \nreturn to our forecast. As the winds aloft will be very light, any\nstorms that do develop will move slowly. In combination with the\nhigh rain rates with these storms due to an abundance of low-level\nmoisture, localized flash flooding will be possible.",
